data.content:
## What breaks in .NET 11

In .NET 10, the Cosmos DB provider already threw `InvalidOperationException` for synchronous LINQ by default, but you could opt back in with the sync-over-async escape hatch (e.g., `EnableSynchronousIO()`). **In .NET 11 (EF Core 11), that opt-in is removed entirely.** There is no way to run sync LINQ against the Cosmos provider.

All four methods will throw `InvalidOperationException` at runtime:

| Sync call | Async replacement |
|---|---|
| `ToList()` | `ToListAsync()` |
| `First(...)` | `FirstAsync(...)` |
| `Count()` | `CountAsync()` |
| `SaveChanges()` | `SaveChangesAsync()` |

## The fix — convert to async

```csharp
public class ProductRepository
{
    private readonly CosmosDbContext _context;

    public async Task<List<Product>> GetAllAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.ToListAsync();
    }

    public async Task<Product> GetByIdAsync(int id)
    {
        return await _context.Products.FirstAsync(p => p.Id == id);
    }

    public async Task<int> GetCountAsync()
    {
        return await _context.Products.CountAsync();
    }

    public async Task SaveAsync(Product product)
    {
        _context.Products.Add(product);
        await _context.SaveChangesAsync();
    }
}
```

**Key points:**
- `Add()` stays synchronous — it only tracks in-memory; no I/O occurs until `SaveChangesAsync()`.
- Callers must be updated to `await` these methods, which will ripple async up the call stack (controllers, services, etc.).
- Remove the `EnableSynchronousIO()` configuration since it no longer compiles.
